<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schemas={schemas} lastUpdated="2026-05-30">
  <article>
    <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href="/learn">Learn</a> / zero vs. Forage</nav>
    <span class="mono-label">COMPARE</span>
    <h1>zero vs. Forage: which is better for getting hired?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">Forage is one of the best ways to explore what a job is actually like, for free, using simulations from real companies. zero is built one step further down the funnel: to get you hired. The deciding difference is whether your work is evaluated against a professional bar and put in front of recruiters, or whether you simply finish with a certificate.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>Forage offers free virtual job simulations from real companies and is excellent for exploring roles before you commit.</li>
        <li>On Forage you finish with a completion certificate. The work itself is generally not scored against a professional standard or reviewed by recruiters.</li>
        <li>On zero, your work is scored against a professional bar and becomes a receipt recruiters can evaluate, and recruiters pay for access to that talent.</li>
        <li>For exploring a role, Forage is great. For proving you can do it and getting found, zero is built for that job.</li>
        <li>Using both is reasonable: explore with Forage, then build evaluated proof with zero.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What Forage does well</h2>
      <p>Give Forage real credit, because it earns it. It took a genuine problem, that students have no idea what most jobs actually involve, and solved it elegantly with free, company-branded simulations. You can spend a few hours doing tasks modeled on the work at a real firm and come away with a much clearer sense of whether that role is for you. That is valuable, it costs nothing, and it shows initiative when you mention it. If your main question is what is this job even like, Forage is one of the best answers available.</p>
      <p>The limit is not effort or quality of the simulations. It is what you walk away with. Forage hands you a certificate of completion. A certificate is a claim that you finished, and like most claims on a résumé, a recruiter has to take it on faith. The actual work you produced, which is the part that could prove you are good, generally stays inside the simulation. You did the work and kept the badge instead of the evidence.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What zero does differently</h2>
      <p>zero starts from the goal Forage stops short of: getting your real work in front of the people who hire. You take on company-shaped tasks in the role you are targeting, you produce the actual deliverable, and your work is scored against the standard strong professionals are held to. You get feedback specific enough to act on, and the scored result becomes a receipt: a piece of evaluated work a recruiter can open and judge before they look at your transcript. Because recruiters pay for access to candidates who have already proven they can do the job, zero stays free for students. The simulation is the method, but the output is hiring signal, not a badge.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Side by side</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Forage</th><th>zero</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cost for students</td><td>Free</td><td>Free</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What you do</td><td>Company-branded task simulations</td><td>Company-shaped tasks in your target role</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What you walk away with</td><td>Completion certificate</td><td>Scored receipt of evaluated work</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scored against a professional bar</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Recruiters evaluate the work</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Yes, it is the point</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Exploring what a role is like</td><td>Proving you can do it and getting found</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Which should you use?</h2>
      <p>It depends on where you are. If you are still figuring out what you want to do, start with Forage and sample a few roles cheaply. The moment you know the direction you are committing to, the question changes from what is this job like to how do I prove I can do it, and that is where zero is built to help. There is no rule that says you pick one. The honest path for most students is to explore with Forage and then build evaluated, recruiter-visible proof with zero, because the second step is the one that actually moves you into a job. Why proof beats a certificate is covered in more depth in our guide on <a href="/learn/how-do-you-prove-you-can-do-the-job-before-you-have-the-job">proving you can do the job before you have it</a>.</p>
    </section>

